# RedMed — project rules for AI coding assistants

Local-first emergency medical ID **iPhone app**. Owner fills in profile data
once; it's written to a **passive** NFC chip (wristband/tag). Another iPhone
with RedMed scans the chip and shows the native emergency card. No backend,
no accounts, nothing ever sent to a server, by design — keep it that way
unless explicitly told to change it.

**Product is iOS-only.** Do not build, revive, or market web owner UI,
Android/TWA, Play listings, or desktop apps as product surfaces. One repo;
the shippable app is `ios/`.

**Passive NFC only:** NTAG-style ISO 14443 tags — no BLE beacons, no active
RFID, no battery-powered bands, no continuous broadcast. Do not add radio
features beyond phone-tap read/write of NDEF URI records.

## Sole repository

This product lives in **one** GitHub repo only:
`https://github.com/MaxRoot1122/RedMed`.

Do not create, recommend, or depend on a second repo. Support is email only
(`help.RedMed@gmail.com`). Pages may host legal pages + `get.html` (App Store
QR); that is not the product UI.

## Where things live

- `ios/RedMed/` — **the RedMed app** (SwiftUI). Open **`ios/RedMed.xcodeproj`**
  in Xcode (NFC capability, `redmed://` URL scheme, bundle ID `local.redmed.app`
  are preconfigured — see `ios/SETUP.md`). All product UI for owners and
  in-app emergency cards lands here. Nothing here has ever been compiled in
  this Linux cloud environment; treat any Swift edit as unverified until
  built on a real Mac.
- `AppConfig.medicalCardBaseURL` — `redmed://card`. New NFC writes are
  `redmed://card#d=<base64url JSON>`. Emergency UI is native
  `ScannedCardView`. Legacy HTTPS `#d=` tags still decode when scanned
  in-app.
- `get.html` — packaging QR → App Store (iPhone only).
- `privacy-policy.html` / `terms-of-service.html` — App Store / legal host.
- `android/` — **retired** (`android/RETIRED.md`). Do not publish.
- `index.html` — legacy HTTPS emergency card for older bracelets only;
  not the owner product. Prefer iOS edits over web owner-tab work.
- `RedMed.app/` — optional Mac helper to launch iOS Simulator.
- `assets/icon.svg` — app cover / home-screen icon source of truth.
- `assets/trauma-hospitals.json` — bundled offline trauma directory for
  Find 911 (keep in sync with `ios/RedMed/trauma-hospitals.json`).
- `docs/TRAUMA_FINDER.md` — agent handoff for trauma center UX.

## Things that will silently break if you miss them

- **Passive NFC only.** Bracelets are **passive NTAG** chips (ISO 14443 Type A,
  NDEF URI). No battery, no BLE, no continuous broadcast. App code: CoreNFC
  write/read only; do not add Bluetooth, UHF, or live-tracking radios.
- **iOS-only NFC opens.** New tags store `redmed://card#d=…`. A phone without
  RedMed installed cannot open the card. Do not reintroduce HTTPS card writes
  or Android/web owner apps without an explicit ask.
- **Never fake satellite-tracking/pointing UI.** No public API exposes real
  satellite position to third-party apps. Real device compass heading
  (`CLLocationManager`) is fine; simulated satellite direction is not.
- **No backend, no cloud sync of profile data.** Don't reintroduce network
  calls carrying profile data without explicit instruction. **Exception:**
  optional Google Maps on Find 911 only.
- If you still edit `index.html` (legacy), recompute its CSP `sha256-` hash
  and keep `RedMed.app/Contents/Resources/www/index.html` byte-identical.

## Conventions

- Comments explain *why*, not *what* — keep that pattern.
- Brace/paren-balance-check any Swift edit (`python3` one-liner counting
  `{`/`}` and `(`/`)`) since there's no Xcode here to catch syntax errors.
